```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 7, 2024
said the standards are the same as for consideration of the initial applications.
Bruce Asay, attorney for the applicant, said he is here to support the request to
reconsider the earlier denial. Asay said he reviewed the video of the last meeting and
had concerns about the comments made. He acknowledged there have been
problems in the past, and he stated attempts were made to address them. He said he
spoke with Terry Bison Ranch, Pro City, Black Cats (aka Wyoming Fireworks
Warehouse), Power Sports and Wholesale, and each person with whom he spoke did
not cite any issues with his clients’ business.
Asay stated his client has requested the opportunity to address problems that have
occurred. He referred to a letter from the county to the applicant a year ago, to which
Dion Custis, attorney for the applicant, had provided a response. Asay said nothing
has been mentioned to the applicant since June 2023.
Asay stated their position is the applicant went through the process, satisfying
requirements including site plan reviews, onsite inspections, and payment of more
than $11,000 in fees. He said his client signed the application affirming they will abide
by county regulations, and no adverse comments about the applications were raised.
He concluded they are entitled to receive the permits.
Commissioner Thompson spoke about an issue in 2019. He said Amy Lenhardt,
Terry Grazing Association, had an agreement with the Elliotts to have a tractor-trailer
on their property. Lenhardt reported to the county sheriff's office her concerns about a
person on top of the trailer with a bullhorn and flag and had expressed fears that
someone might get hurt because of the buffalo in the pasture. Thompson said
Lenhardt’s attorney had a discussion about the safety concerns with Elliott and Custis,
during which, they agreed the individual should get down from the trailer. Thompson
said based on text messages, Elliott and Custis later decided they had a right to be
on the trailer.
Asay noted the complaint was in 2019, applications were approved in subsequent
years, and there has yet to be a single citation within the last year. Thompson asked if
Asay had spoken with Phantom Fireworks. Asay said he had not. Thompson said his
comments at the previous meeting were about the applicant’s established pattern of
behavior. Thompson read a complaint from Phantom Fireworks from a couple of
years ago, which included the applicants’ business driving up and down the road,
obstructing traffic, using a bullhorn, and fear for the safety of Phantom’s employees
and customers. The complaint cited employees' concerns that they felt unsafe walking
to their vehicles. Asay noted that concern was raised years ago. He said the applicant
did not go into the streets and the sheriff's office addressed the bullhorn issue, clearly
stating the applicant had not violated the law. Asay said his clients’ aggressive
marketing is effective and brings revenue to the county.
