### Page 8 of 16

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 7, 2024
Brianna Elliott, applicant, requested her applications be approved. She spoke about
the positive impact the businesses have had in Laramie County, contributing
significantly to the county's tax base, including substantial sales tax revenue and
attracting customers to the area. She pointed to application fees she has paid and a
nonrefundable commitment to purchase fireworks. Elliott noted when a letter of
concern came from the county, those concerns were addressed. She said a citation
had not been issued within the last year and there were no further letters from the
county. Elliott said her business competes for customers and needs to bring attention
to their site. She said she acts by the law, and if there are concerns, bring them to her
attention and allow her to address them. Elliot said only Artillery World uses the
speaker, and it is used appropriately. She said she believes other events, such as
concerts at Terry Bison and the Daddy of 'em All, impact noise more. She stated she
looks forward to open communications, transparency, and collaboration with Laramie
County officials. Elliott said the sheriff's office has repeatedly informed them they are
working within the confines of the law.
Chairman Lovett opened the hearing for PUBLIC COMMENT. Petra Goldberg,
representative of Phantom Fireworks, spoke about what she described as constant
bombardment from the applicant, their activity in the easement, use of a bullhorn,
complaints from her customers, removal of people from her showroom who were
disrupting customers, damage to her fireworks and removal of her price tags, and
employees and their parents feeling it is unsafe to work in the environment. She
requested the board not reconsider giving permits to the applicant.
Matt Butler, Fire Warden, spoke about complaints that have come to his attention, but
which are not directly from the source. He said the major concerns and complaints
were use of the bullhorn, people in the middle of the street and on top of trailers. He
said he looks at these issues from the safety point of view and if they distract drivers.
Custis believes there needs to be more communication about what is or is not
allowed. He said he has addressed complaints brought to his attention. He noted
some of the complaints presented by Goldberg have yet to be addressed to him. He
said he believes Phantom has made false allegations of assault. Custis said there
have not been any hearings or validation of these allegations and complaints. On the
subject of the bullhorn, he said his client was told one thing by the sheriff's office and
another by the board. Custis reiterated if complaints are sent to him, he will address
them with his client for compliance under the law. He requested the board reconsider
their denial of the permits.
Thompson spoke about a letter from the County Attorney dated June 15, 2023,
referencing complaints the county had received. Thompson said communication has
not just been with the sheriff's office, but also with the county attorney's office. He
asked why Custis would take what the sheriff's office says over what county
commissioners say. Thompson questioned if Custis had shared their concerns about
