```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 7, 2024
his behavior and the citation of state statute. Custis repeated he would have
addressed what is or is not acceptable with his client.
Commissioner Malm spoke about emails commissioners received on April 16 and
17, 2024. Malm asked if Custis would deem those emails as threats. Custis said he
did not feel the emails were threats and spoke about his client’s feelings of being
discriminated against and believes the board assumed the validity of complaints
without an opportunity for response. Custis noted he feels the emails were sent in
frustration, and the only recourse is a legal remedy. Malm said he perceived the
emails as a threat to his colleagues. Malm noted he voted for some of the
applications and intended to put some conditions in place at the previous meeting,
but in light of the emails, he will vote no. Malm spoke about Custis' client, Frank Elliott,
and the applicant, Brianna Elliott, pointing out this is the first time the board has heard
from Brianna. He said communication is usually from her father, Frank, who is not
actually a party to the application. Custis said his client was advised to have
communications go to the county through the attorney.
Commissioner Holmes described an incident last year when he felt intimidated being
recorded by a representative of Custis’ client on a public right-of-way after Holmes
had observed activity in the area. Custis said his client feels he is under investigation
and needs to record interactions with competitors and some board members.
Aaron Veldheer, sheriff's chief deputy of operations, noted the office received several
calls about bullhorns, signs, and a fire truck driving up and down the road with lights
and sirens activated. He said he could not go into great detail about the emails
because of an active investigation.
Hearing no further PUBLIC COMMENT, Chairman Lovett closed the hearing.
Voss presented documents as exhibits to support the items mentioned today.
A motion was made by Vice Chairman Malm, seconded by Commissioner
Thompson, to reconsider the application.
Thompson discussed a letter from Custis, in which Custis discussed constitutional
problems with reliance on third-party complaints that have not been proven or their
ability to respond.
Thompson explained the board is not a court and said he does not feel the legislature
intended for the board to be comprised of attorneys. He feels the board is a
representative of the community, and its members were elected to make decisions.
Thompson said he needs to take all the information about decisions and what has
been witnessed firsthand. He pointed to complaints that businesses are being
threatened and harmed, and took into consideration all the information, including the
fact that the county attorney had sent multiple letters about issues. Thompson recalled
