### Page 10 of 16

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 7, 2024
a time he witnessed a person in the right-of-way with a bullhorn trying to flag him down.
He talked about the danger of having an old fire truck running up and down the road
and a person on top of a trailer. Thompson said there was a threat from the sheriff's
office to trespass if the person did not get off the trailer. He said the issue is not
obnoxious marketing; it is health and safety. Thompson stated it is the board's job to
make sure our citizens can drive down the road and not be harassed by somebody in
the right-of-way and to run a business without employees being scared for their safety.
Thompson added that even with all the letters sent to the applicant, the only recourse
is for the board to deny the permit.
Holmes said the board is charged with the health, welfare, and safety of the
community. He stated what he observed on two different occasions was not good for
the community.
Malm spoke about trying to put some restrictions on the permit at the last meeting to
see if they could be good operators. He said there could be a disagreement about the
tone of the emails, but in his opinion, they were threatening. He said until Frank Elliott
is not a party to this transaction or license and there are assurances he will not
participate in the business, Malm cannot vote for the permit. Malm addressed Elliott's
claim of discrimination, noting there is not a single Wyoming-based operator, and the
issue is not a Colorado vs Wyoming thing.
Commissioner Heath said if she had a young teenager learning to work, the last thing
she would want them to learn is unethical business practices. Health and safety are
concerns. She spoke about treating your neighbor and fellow businessmen right.
Heath said if the board hears a complaint, it is general practice to investigate the
complaint, and it is not unusual to go out and observe.
The motion failed by the following vote:
Lovett, Malm, Heath, Holmes, Thompson
Nay:
